# Introduction

<p class="lead">This is where the Llama brand, <strong>Llama UI</strong> and <strong>Llama Chat</strong> are designed, built and checked. Everything in it is the real component, font or file, so what you see here is what ships.</p>

## In short

- **What:** the brand (the icon, Llama Sans and Llama Mono, the colours), the component library Llama UI, the app Llama Chat built from it, and the communications made with them.
- **Who it's for:** people who design or build Llama products, and the agents that work with them.
- **Why one place:** the brand, the UI and the posts make the same decisions, so they're shown side by side and checked against each other.

## What it's for

- **Designing:** try a new screen, post or asset against the real system before it ships.
- **Building:** look up a component, its states and its tokens, in light and dark.
- **Checking:** every story renders the real code, and the accessibility addon checks each one.
- **Handing over:** the rules for agents (**Agents**) are exported, with **Communications**, as the Llama design system for Claude Design, by `npm run export:design-system`.

## How we think

The short version of **Philosophy**:

- **Local, on ordinary hardware:** the model runs on your own machine, one people already have; the conversations stay on it, and it works offline, without an account.
- **Simple by default, deep on request:** the apps start with one suggested model, set up for you; every other setting is one step away rather than on the first screen.
- **Recognisable, easy to change:** the character is in three places, the icon, the details of the type and one accent, cinder. Everything else is neutral, so an app can bring its own colour.
- **Function first:** every element has a job and a reason for its size. The interface stays out of the way of the conversation.
- **Design lives in code:** the tokens, in three tiers, are the design. Change a primitive and the rest follows.

The reasons are in **Ideology**, **Product** and **Design**, under **Philosophy**.

## How it fits together

- **llama.brand** is the one source of the icon, the fonts and the colours.
- **llama.cpp** runs the models. Its server, llama-server, bundles **Llama Chat**, built from **Llama UI** in `tools/ui`, which is this Storybook's home.
- **Llama for Mac** and **Llama for Windows** run llama-server and open Llama Chat in their own window.
- **llama.app**, the website, shows models the way Llama Chat does.

<Figure n={1} caption="The products and their repositories. llama.brand supplies the brand; llama.cpp holds the engine and Llama UI; the native apps run the engine and open Llama Chat.">
	<Products />
</Figure>

Inside Llama UI, the tokens go from primitives to semantic roles to components, in `src/lib/styles/tokens/`, and `DESIGN.md` is generated from them. Llama UI draws the interface and handles interaction; each app owns its own state.

## How it's laid out

- **Introduction:** this page.
- **Agents:** **Design guidelines** (`DESIGN.md`) and **Brand guidelines** (`BRAND.md`), the rules written for agents first: precise rather than persuasive.
- **Philosophy:** **Ideology**, **Product** and **Design**, the why, written for people.
- **Brand:** the logo, from how the icon is drawn to its files, the typography and the colours.
- **Communications:** how posts, store screenshots, link previews and swag are made, from the direction and the grid to the finished pieces.
- **UI:** the foundations, every component from actions to overlays, the patterns, and the blocks, Llama Chat's real screens. Proposals are ideas not yet in the app.

## Working in it

- Run it with `npm run storybook` in `tools/ui`.
- Pages that are mostly prose, like this one, are `.svx`: Markdown with Svelte components in it. `AGENTS.md` says how to write them.
- Where a guideline and a story disagree, the story is right: it's the real thing.
